{"id": "GLOBAL-learning-04:semantic:1871:29796cdd", "text": "An analyst comparing revisions should be able to reproduce both the original and revised values using the corresponding source events and rule versions. A file with the same name and a different modification time is insufficient lineage. Each revision has a new checksum and a declared cutoff, even if only one event changed. Where a revision affects several campaigns, list them explicitly rather than implying that every campaign in the same period changed.

Reviewers distinguish a data correction from a methodology change. Correcting a mistyped anchor date applies an existing rule to better evidence. Replacing a thirty-day window with a different window changes the rule and therefore the meaning of the count. A methodology change needs a separate comparison note and cannot be presented as a routine data cleanup. If both occur together, calculate their effects separately when the data permits. If their effects cannot be separated, state that limitation. A single combined difference should not be attributed entirely to delivery performance when part of it comes from a changed definition.

Downstream consumers receive a revision notice identifying superseded artifacts and the scope of the change. A cached chart, extracted table, or presentation image may continue to show the old values after the source file is corrected."}

